WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.280
Clear voice notes, photos that explain, and short how-to videos.

2
00:00:04.630 --> 00:00:09.770
This is the video for module two of Voice Notes, Photos and Videos for Expression.

3
00:00:11.020 --> 00:00:15.430
Most unclear voice notes have one problem: the point comes at the very end.

4
00:00:16.680 --> 00:00:24.940
This module gives you one method for each format: a voice note with the point first, photos that explain, and a short planned video.

5
00:00:26.190 --> 00:00:29.410
Every lesson ends with a real message that you send.

6
00:00:30.660 --> 00:00:40.300
Part one. After a short greeting, say in one sentence why you are sending this. For example: I am calling about the date for Ada's naming ceremony.

7
00:00:41.550 --> 00:00:46.930
Part two. Two or three facts only. If there are more, it may need a call or a typed list.

8
00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:54.430
Part three. Say what you want the listener to do, and by when. Please tell me by Friday if Saturday works.

9
00:00:55.680 --> 00:01:01.420
Then close with a short thanks or blessing, and stop. Do not trail off with OK, so, yes.

10
00:01:02.670 --> 00:01:05.590
So think for ten seconds, then put the point first.

11
00:01:06.840 --> 00:01:21.090
For example, Grace in Ibadan is treasurer of her church women's group. She must tell 40 members that contributions will be due on a new date. Her first recording lasts 2 minutes 40 seconds, and the new date comes only at the end.

12
00:01:22.340 --> 00:01:28.170
So she plans. The point: from next month, contributions are due on the 5th, not the 10th.

13
00:01:29.420 --> 00:01:35.450
The details: the bank charges them when the hall fee is paid late, and the account has not changed.

14
00:01:36.700 --> 00:01:40.630
The next step: please reply with a thumbs-up so I know you heard this.

15
00:01:41.880 --> 00:01:52.350
Her new note is 38 seconds, with the new date typed underneath. 31 of the 40 members reply with a thumbs-up, and only two ask her to repeat the date.

16
00:01:53.600 --> 00:01:59.510
Move away from the generator, television and fan. Hold the phone a hand's length from your mouth.

17
00:02:00.760 --> 00:02:06.740
Hold the microphone button and slide up to lock. Speak a little slower, and pause between parts.

18
00:02:07.990 --> 00:02:13.060
Tap stop, not send. Play it back. Then send, or tap the bin and record again.

19
00:02:14.310 --> 00:02:27.750
A useful photo answers a question before anyone asks it. First, light. Use daylight, with the light falling on the subject, not behind it. Avoid the flash, which makes faces flat and causes glare.

20
00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:40.390
Second, framing. Wipe the lens, because a fingerprint smear is the most common cause of hazy photos. Walk closer instead of zooming, keep it level, and clear the background.

21
00:02:41.640 --> 00:02:51.440
Third, scale. A photo alone cannot show size. Put a coin, your hand or a tape measure beside it. A tape measure is best, because it gives a number.

22
00:02:52.690 --> 00:03:07.020
The fourth idea is sequence: wide, medium, close-up. For example, Bisi in Abeokuta wants a tailor 20 kilometres away to copy a dress. She hangs it near a window and takes a wide shot of the whole dress.

23
00:03:08.270 --> 00:03:15.250
Then a medium shot of the neckline and sleeves, and a close-up of the zip and pleats, with a tape measure across the waist.

24
00:03:16.500 --> 00:03:26.290
She sends them in order and types: length 112 cm, same neckline. The tailor asks just one question, instead of asking her to come in.

25
00:03:27.540 --> 00:03:39.810
Use video only when movement, order or sound is the message. For example, Ngozi in Lugbe, Abuja, writes a shot list of four landmarks from the main road to her yellow gate.

26
00:03:41.060 --> 00:03:48.690
Her brother drives while she films from the passenger seat, holding the phone with both hands, naming each landmark as it appears.

27
00:03:49.940 --> 00:03:59.740
Her raw video is 3 minutes 10 seconds. She trims the slow parts in her gallery editor down to 1 minute 25 seconds, and watches it once with sound.

28
00:04:00.990 --> 00:04:10.570
She sends it with a location pin and the typed address. And remember: never film while driving. Ask a passenger to film, or stop safely first.

29
00:04:11.820 --> 00:04:25.150
To remember. Point first, with the key fact typed underneath. Listen back before you send. Use daylight, move closer and add scale, then send wide, medium and close-up. Keep videos short and trimmed.

30
00:04:26.400 --> 00:04:38.370
Now practise with real messages. Record, review and resend one voice note. Send a three-photo explanation with a typed line. And make a one-minute how-to video with a shot list.

31
00:04:38.720 --> 00:04:40.660
Continue with the lessons below.
